          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
            over real or complex numbers appear as the linearization of real or complex Lie groups; the infinitesimal version of Lie groups are
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lie+group"} local Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie%27s+three+theorems"} Lie's three theorems
            and the study of this correspondence is the classical Lie theory in narrow sense.
          %p
            In a categorical context there are the usual two generalization of Lie groups: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+categorification"} horizontal categorification
            – or oidification – which adds
            %em more objects
            and leads to
            = succeed ";" do
              %em Lie groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
            which adds
            %em higher morphisms
            and leads to
            = succeed "-Lie" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
