          %h3#lie_1groupoids_vs_lie_algebroids Lie 1-groupoids vs. Lie algebroids
          %p
            The study of Lie theory of Lie groupoids and Lie algebroids was notably motivated by Alan Weinstein’s studies of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kirill+Mackenzie"} Kirill Mackenzie's
            work.
          %p
            The canonical review for the integration theory of Lie algebroids (and hence for Lie algebras by the
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong path integration method
          %ul
            %li
              Marius Crainic, Rui Loja Fernandes,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on Integrability of Lie Brackets
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://aps.arxiv.org/abs/math.DG/0611259"} arXiv
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/05/integrability_of_lie_brackets.html"} blog
          %p
            This focuses on integration to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoids
            proper, i.e. to integration internal to manifolds. In contrast to Lie algebras, not every Lie algebroid integrates to such a proper
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            %a{:href => "http://aps.arxiv.org/abs/math.DG/0611259"} discuss
            the obstruction in detail.) But the quotient of course always exists as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+quotient"} weak quotient
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacky+quotient"} stacky quotient
            itself. The result of realizing the space of morphisms of the integrated Lie algebroid as such a stacky quotient has been studied by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "http://chenchangzhu.blogspot.com/"} Chenchang Zhu
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory+for+stacky+Lie+groupoids"} Lie theory for stacky Lie groupoids
          %h3#higher_categorical Higher categorical
          %p
            See at
